steps.message:
I fixed it: rotated objects on the terrace were coming out at the wrong angle and in the wrong place because of a unit-conversion bug in the client's mesh rotation code. The fix is one line. It compiles and links, but I haven't run the game to check the terrace by eye.

**What the video shows:** the fence pieces placed with no rotation look right. The ones that were turned are pointing the wrong way, and some lie flat on the ground.

**Cause:** in `src/client/mesh.cpp`, `rotateMesh()` (which all the mesh-rotation helpers call) converted the angle with `core::radToDeg(degrees)` where it should have used `core::degToRad(degrees)`. So a 90° turn became about 5157 radians, a meaningless angle. The rotation is about the node's centre, so a wrong angle also moves parts of the model, which is why objects showed up in the wrong place as well as facing the wrong way. Unrotated objects skip this code, which is why they looked fine.

```diff
-	degrees = core::radToDeg(degrees);
+	degrees = core::degToRad(degrees);
```

**Why this covers every case, not just the video:** this is the single rotation routine for mesh-drawn objects, wherever they are in the world. The fix applies to all of these:
- any facing direction set with facedir or colored facedir (all 24), whether the rotated version comes from the cache or is built on the spot (smooth lighting builds it on the spot);
- objects attached to walls, floors or ceilings (wallmounted);
- objects turned by a free angle (degrotate);
- the angled preview of items held in the hand (`wieldmesh.cpp`), which goes through the same routine.

I checked the lookup tables that turn placement into a rotation, and the code that positions objects within a map block. Both are correct, so this conversion was the only thing wrong in that path.
